    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Articular cartilage, a smooth white tissue composed of chondrocytes, cushions the ends of joints, yet possesses very poor regenerative potential. Identifying novel factors that govern cartilage development is an essential foundation for developing stem and progenitor cell-based therapies. Such therapies could include using cartilage regeneration to address focal cartilage injuries as well as osteoarthritis, a debilitating disease brought about by loss of cartilage tissue. Previous work has elucidated the role of the Wnt signaling pathway, a key regulator of gene transcription, in the process of chondrogenesis, or cartilage differentiation. We seek to address the specific role of one of the pathway’s non-canonical receptors: the leucine-rich repeat-containing G-protein coupled receptor (Lgr).  The specific member Lgr5 was chosen as the focus of our study because of its previous implication as an adult stem cell marker. This trait is of particular interest to us in the field of progenitor cell-based therapies for cartilage tissue. We sought to investigate the expression and role of Lgr5 in cartilage in order to understand and identify tissue-resident stem or progenitor cells in the tissue. Surprisingly, we found that Lgr5 expression increased during chondrogenesis, and its knockdown significantly impaired this process. Using global microarray-based gene and protein expression assays to analyze the chondroprogenitor cell line ATDC5, we found that the Lgr5 knockdown led to decreased expression of the known chondrogenic markers Type II Collagen (Col2a1) and Aggrecan (Agc). Additionally, we observe that Wnt3a dependent β-catenin is activated during chondroprogenitor differentiation in parallel to Lgr5 upregulation. Further research towards understanding the molecular mechanisms underlying cartilage differentiation will aid in unlocking the tissue’s regenerative potential and help define future clinical strategies for enhancing cartilage regeneration.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
